<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Filet talks HTTP, in plain text, to two things and nothing else:

  1. **Another phone on the same network**, serving its shared set.
  2. **This device's own server**, which a browser on the LAN opens.

  NEARBY.md §4.1 explains why that transport is plain HTTP rather than HTTPS: a self-signed
  certificate makes every browser show a full-page "Your connection is not private"
  interstitial, and half the people who see it stop. The peer↔peer side pins its own
  certificate, so it needs no CA; the browser side has no way to do that.

  Android has no syntax for "private address ranges only", so this is a base config rather
  than something narrower. What keeps it honest is that the app SAYS SO, in the share screen,
  in one line: "Transfer is not encrypted on this network."

  No other part of Filet makes an HTTP request. There is no telemetry, no ads and no
  analytics, so there is nothing else this could weaken.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
